solutions.latex:
\exfigure{1035_q11e_s1}

Asymptotes: $x=0$ and $y=4$ (as $x\to\infty$, $\dfrac{3}{x}\to 0$, so $y\to 4$).

$x$-intercept: let $y=0$, i.e. $\dfrac{4x-3}{x}=0$, so $4x-3=0$ and $x=\dfrac{3}{4}$.

\exfigure{1035_q13c_s1}

There are three points of intersection, as shown in the diagram above.

OR: When $x<0$ or $x>8$, $y=1-\dfrac{x}{4}$ is outside the range $[-1,1]$ of $y=\sin x$ and hence only intersects three times in $0<x<8$.
